\f1\fs22 \'93Hello Chanta, my old mate,\'94 Alexander said, blinking with that slow, unhurried calm that won him so many fans.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta squeezed the pain from his eyes until his vision aligned with the sound of the familiar voice. \'93Alexander? What the tax? Why\'97?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m here to deliver you from the pickle jar. Or throw you in; I haven\'92t decided yet.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta backed against the suburban door, his pelvis grinding against the wheel arch. The synapses of his aching brain burst like popcorn in a campfire as he tried to process why Alexander would be threatening him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I had a whole day of production meetings and package editing today\'94 Alexander complained. \'93I have some truly reprehensible Cloudborne boofheads for this week\'92s 
\f2\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0 . Yet, here I am again, running investigative errands for the Commissioner, only this time, I find you\'92re at the heart of them.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What are you talking about?\'94 Chanta shunted his panicked thoughts up a gear, and achieved the break-necked speeds of a cement wagon with a bustle axle. \'93Look, if it\'92s about that suit I nearly ran over a few weeks\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander dismissed Chanta\'92s fumbled words with a wave of his paw-like hand and a knot in his furry eyebrows. \'93Don\'92t make it worse for yourself, my dear friend. We already have quite enough to lock you in a cell, and throw away the whole prison.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A look of horror salted Chanta\'92s face. \'93What are\'97who\'97?\'94 he stammered until Alexander\'92s narrowing eyes and thinning lips drained him of bluster.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93All right,\'94 Alexander said with a shrug that made him look both smaller and somehow more immovable. \'93Polish it that way, if you must.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why you?\'94 Chanta pleaded. \'93Why does it have to be you?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why me, ever, Chanta? The same reason as always: they want my rubber stamp on a fix-up that no one will ever look beyond.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Everyone is afraid of your mother.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s gaze softened, his round face settling into cuddlier lines\'97habit over honesty. \'93Quite so, and it\'92s an unceasing curse, I assure you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A curse?\'94 Chanta demanded, nearly launching into a litany of how Alexander benefited from privilege. He cramped up, as if his intestines had slithered into his throat and tied his tongue in a bow. Eloise Shakti infamously forced a school to redact \'91Monkeys retain information better\'92 from his report card. She rescued and repainted the Roger Rogerson Police Academy just to get him admitted\'97and still he couldn\'92t shoot straight with a \'91bag and tag\'92 Particle Pulse Pistol.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s only truly innate skills were smiling, talking to the camera, and capitulating whenever a load threatened to be heavier than a feather. As the son of Eloise Shakti, Alexander\'92s social status dwarfed everyone else\'92s, Commissioner included.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta tried not to be bitter about it. After all, serving as friend and cleaner for the world\'92s most pampered, independently wealthy fool was, in some sideways logic, a career highlight.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just be thankful,\'94 Alexander injected into the uncomfortable pause as Chanta ground his back teeth nearly to dust, \'93they sent me to round you up, and not the PAPER agents you beat up in that seedy Memphis Mafia Bar.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What?\'94 Chanta gasped, incredulous. \'93They're the ones\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No use arguing with me about it, my recidivating pal. PAPER\'92s not about to admit they were bested by a cleaner, unless it was true. Let\'92s see what we have\'85 stealing some highly-classified gadget, conspiracy with those sage accomplices, Travis Teh\'92s murder\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Who?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The shotgun.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You said it was suicide!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Officially, yes. But we know better, right? Very clever of you, Chanta\'97all these suspicious incidents have one key thing in common: you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They're all coincidences! Alex, see reason.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Reason is the last gambit of a scoundrel, Chanta. What about the PAPER agent murdered with some kind of triangular ice pick?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I have no idea\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93She was at the Stickett Inn, the night you blew it up.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I can't have murdered her. You saw me lying injured after the blast.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, so you don\'92t deny blowing up the Inn.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"But I do deny it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How do you know it was an ice pick?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You just told me!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why so defensive? The security cams show no evidence of us picking you up.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You did pick me up\'97me and Sarah!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s nostrils flared at Sarah\'92s name, then he seemed to slip into koala-still reverie.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Alexander!\'94 Chanta snapped his fingers. \'93Alex!\'94 He came back. \'93Surely you remember kicking me out onto the hospital roof?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And what was the hospital?\'94 Alexander pounced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta stalled, memory blank.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93St Gertha\'92s Sacred Goitre? Our Lady of Perpetual Infection?\'94 Alexander prosecuted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Um, the Perpetual Infection one.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ha! It was Our Lady of the Sacred Goitre.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So you 
\f2\i did
\f1\i0  roll me out of the cruiser.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And you lied about the name.\'94 Alexander\'92s eyes narrowed. \'93Which makes me wonder, maybe neither of us was ever there.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Have you gone mad?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93If I have, it\'92s someone else\'92s doing\'97implanted memories, maybe those missing Sages of yours. Don\'92t you think it\'92s suspicious how they\'92ve disappeared off the Travelator?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta sagged, fists tight. \'93I\'92m totally taxed, aren\'92t I?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander laid a soft, round hand on Chanta\'92s thigh, more creeping-familiar than comforting. \'93Look, Chanta. For a grainy, Layer-soaked Cloudborne, you\'92re all right. You\'92ve cleaned me out of some real troubles.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta managed a bitter laugh. \'93Some stains that would make chemists and priests retire.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why am I arresting you? I\'92m not. I\'92m just trying to see how much trouble you\'92re in. Given your confessions\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I haven\'92t confessed to anything!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander chuckled and squeezed Chanta\'92s thigh a little too tenderly. \'93Good. Keep it up, maybe you\'92ll only get five or six decades for this mess.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Five or six\'97?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, I know, sounds cruel. But the Cloudborne need firm management. Chanta, I really do want to help. I have\'85 influence.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You mean your mother.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander retracted as if he\'92d tasted something sour. \'93That\'92s not fair, Chanta. I\'92m genuinely trying to help.\'94 He let the silence hang, only Chanta\'92s teeth grinding filling it in. \'93But there is something. Something you could do for me. Something good, Chanta, the most beautiful thing you\'92ve ever managed in your miserable, sunless little life.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta swore under his breath and kept silent. Maybe six decades in prison was better.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You see,\'94 Alexander cooed, as Chanta\'92s alarm peaked, \'93I\'92ve fallen in love.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What the tax?\'94 Chanta said, almost with relief.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I know. She\'92s perfect, Chanta. Per-fect.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, um, that\'92s nice, Alex. I\'92m\'85 happy for you?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander became solemn, his hands limp and as warm as a marsupial pouch as he clasped Chanta\'92s. \'93Chanta, please. Tell me where you\'92re keeping Sarah Jacobson.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s blood ran cold. There was no world in which he\'92d give Sarah up to Alexander. Even if his intentions were pure, Sarah had as much to hide as him. Tax! Alexander might want to apprehend her too. \'93Um, who?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Come now, Chanta. You know Sarah. She\'92d have died on that street if not for me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So you admit you rescued us from the bombing! We\'92re innocent. Who waits for their own bomb?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That means Sarah\'92s innocent. You, not so sure. But I know Sarah and I were meant to meet. Now, Chanta, which side of my influence do you want to be on?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta wondered what was worse: being Alexander\'92s co-conspirator, or his competition.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s demeanour stiffened. \'93I\'92m a nice guy, Chanta. A catch. Rich, which helps. And very good with women. I can locate a cervix in thirty seconds, even in the dark. It\'92s inconceivable she wouldn\'92t thank me\'97for all the flowers, muffins, vibrators after her accident. She 
\f2\i must
\f1\i0  have been kidnapped, and I\'92m sure it was you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta knew he was properly taxed, caught between a padded cell and Alexander\'92s devotion. \'93I don\'92t know where she is.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Finding Sarah is more important than my exceedingly valuable bachelorhood.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander stopped pawing Chanta\'92s thigh, banged the roof, and the driver pulled over.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What\'92s happening?\'94 Chanta was spooked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander stepped out. \'93No cause for alarm, Chanta. I know exactly who you are.\'94 He winked, touched his nose, and watched the suburban screech away.
